@prefix ldh:    <https://w3id.org/atomgraph/linkeddatahub#> .
@prefix rdf:    <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:   <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix dh:     <https://w3id.org/atomgraph/linkeddatahub/document-hierarchy#> .
@prefix dct:    <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

<> a dh:Item ;
    dct:title "SPARQL endpoint" ;
    dct:description "Query the dataspace using the SPARQL 1.1 Protocol" ;
    rdf:_1 <#content> .

<#content> a ldh:XHTML ;
    rdf:value """<div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
    <p class="ldh-body-lg">Query the dataspace using the SPARQL 1.1 Protocol</p>
    <div>
        <h2 id="sparql">Executing SPARQL</h2>
        <p>Every LinkedDataHub dataspace provides a SPARQL endpoint on <code>sparql</code> path (relative to the dataspace's base URI). It supports the
            <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql11-protocol/" target="_blank">SPARQL 1.1 Protocol</a> and serves as a proxy for the backend endpoint of the
            dataspace.</p>
        <p>A <code>GET</code> on the endpoint without a <code>query</code> parameter returns its
            <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql11-service-description/" target="_blank">SPARQL 1.1 Service Description</a>, which advertises queries only:
            documents are written through the <a href="../documents/">Graph Store Protocol</a>, not SPARQL Update.</p>
        <p>External clients are limited in how many queries they may have in flight and how often they may send them; a query beyond the
            <a href="../../configuration/#sparql-limits">limits</a> is answered with <code>429 Too Many Requests</code>.</p>
        <p>A query result is bounded by <a href="../../configuration/#http"><samp>MAX_QUERY_RESPONSE_LENGTH</samp></a> (5 MiB by default). A longer one is refused, not truncated:
            the response is <code>502 Bad Gateway</code> with a body typed <code>ldh:ResponseTooLarge</code>, and a client that needs the whole result pages through it with
            <code>LIMIT</code>/<code>OFFSET</code>. Behind the platform, the triplestore abandons such a query once its result outgrows the
            <a href="../../configuration/#varnish">Varnish store kept for SPARQL results</a>.</p>
        <p>The protocol's dataset parameters can be used to scope a query to a single document's graph: passing <code>default-graph-uri=&lt;document-uri&gt;</code>
            makes that document's named graph the query's default graph. The dataset specified this way overrides any <code>FROM</code>/<code>FROM NAMED</code>
            clauses in the query, and <code>GRAPH</code> patterns match nothing since no named graphs are part of the dataset. This is the read-side counterpart
            of the graph-scoped <code>PATCH</code> method. Reading the Chai product's name out of its document alone:</p>
        <pre>curl -k -G "https://localhost:4443/sparql" \\
  -H "Accept: application/sparql-results+json" \\
  --data-urlencode 'query=PREFIX schema: &lt;https://schema.org/&gt; SELECT ?name WHERE { ?product a schema:Product ; schema:name ?name }' \\
  --data-urlencode 'default-graph-uri=https://localhost:4443/products/1/'</pre>
    </div>
    <div>
        <h2 id="caching">Caching</h2>
        <p>An anonymous query — one sent without a WebID certificate or an authenticated session — is cached by the front Varnish on its body as well as its URL, whether it arrives as
            <code>GET</code> or as the protocol's <code>POST</code> forms, so the views of a public dataspace reach the platform once per query rather than once per visitor.
            A result is kept for an hour at most, and any write through the platform drops every cached result. Authenticated queries are not cached, since their results
            depend on the agent's access.</p>
    </div>
</div>"""^^rdf:XMLLiteral ;
        rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql11-protocol/> .

    <https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql11-protocol/> dct:title "SPARQL 1.1 Protocol" .
